# agentic-sdlc — shared-layer manifest
#
# The SHARED LAYER is the part of this kit that is copied VERBATIM into your
# project and is not yours to edit. It carries a version so that a project
# bootstrapped from kit 0.1.0 can later be diffed against kit 0.3.0 and told
# exactly what moved. `UPDATING.md` is that recipe; this manifest is the anchor
# it diffs against.
#
# Everything NOT listed here is yours from the moment bootstrap stamps it:
# AGENTS.md (and the CLAUDE.md / GEMINI.md shims beside it), README.md,
# docs/diary.md, docs/adr/, docs/domain-glossary.md, your adapters, the
# .github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md — and, importantly, the POLICY files, which
# are meant to grow with your repo:
#
#   scripts/docs-conformance/config.mjs            what the docs gate enforces
#   scripts/docs-conformance/local-vocabulary.mjs  your product's vocabulary
#   scripts/docs-conformance/test/                 fixture tests; extend freely
#   scripts/guards.config.sh                       the guards' view of your layout
#   scripts/agents.config.sh                       tier -> model, your provider's
#
# Edit those freely — the whole point of stamping a template is that the copy
# is then yours.
#
# THIS FILE IS COPIED WHOLESALE during an update (see UPDATING.md step 5): it
# carries no project-specific content, only which release you are on and what
# that release covers. It is deliberately not listed under `files:` — a manifest
# cannot be an entry in itself.
#
# Versioning, pre-1.0: the MINOR bumps whenever the set of shared files or their
# content changes, so a consumer can always ask "is my layer byte-identical to
# the release I claim?" and get a yes/no. There is no patch-level for prose.
# The split is deliberate: the validator ENGINE is shared because a fix to it
# should reach every project, while the RULES are local because they are the
# thing each project must own and review.
#
# `scripts/check.sh` verifies every file below still exists.

# 0.3.0 — the manual became AGENTS.md. Two shared-layer files changed content:
# scripts/check.sh now requires AGENTS.md as the root manual, and the
# claude-md-refs validator defaults to it and gained the `shim-invalid` rule for
# the tool entry points beside it. No file joined or left the layer.
# 0.4.0 — feedback wave 1: scripts/agents.lib.sh (the capability-tier resolver)
# joined the layer, and /implement gained the Deliver phase (push → PR →
# independent review; skills are not manifest-listed, but the shared invariants
# they cite are unchanged). UPDATING.md changed content too, and materially: it
# now has a SECOND half covering everything this manifest does NOT list. That
# was not cosmetic — this release is the first one whose value sits mostly
# outside the shared layer, so a consumer following the old recipe would have
# taken the resolver above and nothing that reads or calls it.
# 0.5.0 — constitution/shared-code-craft.md joined the layer: ten portable
# rules for the code an agent writes, from the smallest sufficient diff to
# diagrams drawn as SVG in HTML reports, never ASCII art. Two shared files
# changed content with it: scripts/check.sh's reduced fallback now skips the
# new article the way it skips shared-invariants.md, and UPDATING.md now
# documents what an article joining the layer does — Part 1 alone ends with a
# RED gate (article-unreferenced) until the consumer's own manual points at
# the new article; deliberate, and the worked examples are re-captured showing
# it. The wave's non-manifest half, recovered by Part 2: /tdd, /implement and
# /review-pr point at the article, the manual and local-engineering templates
# carry the pointers, /improve-codebase-architecture's report rendering became
# self-contained inline SVG, and the starter config.mjs (yours, step 9d) adds
# the article to the portability file set — existing consumers merge that hunk
# by hand.
# 0.6.0 — the config-discovery trust wave: the loaders stopped treating "the
# repository the process is standing in" as an identity. scripts/guards.lib.sh
# sources the cwd repo's guards.config.sh only when that repo is also the one
# the calling guard lives in (a config is sourced, i.e. CODE), refuses with a
# stderr note otherwise, discovers nothing without an anchor, and scrubs the
# GIT_DIR/GIT_WORK_TREE a git hook inherits so a linked-worktree push cannot
# make a repo's own config look foreign. scripts/agents.lib.sh (the same hole,
# fixed in the sibling PR of this wave) anchors its discovery on the library's
# own location. No file joined or left the layer; both changes are content
# changes to manifest-listed files, which is what this MINOR records.
# 0.7.0 — task-domain routing. No file joined or left the layer; ONE shared file
# changed content: scripts/agents.lib.sh's resolver gained an optional second
# argument, the task DOMAIN, so `sh scripts/agents.lib.sh implementer content`
# reads AGENT_TIER_IMPLEMENTER_CONTENT and falls back to AGENT_TIER_IMPLEMENTER
# when it is unset. Called with one argument it is byte-for-byte the 0.6.0
# behaviour, which is what makes this wave safe to adopt without touching a
# single caller. The two vocabularies are deliberately opposite: the four tiers
# stay CLOSED (an unknown one is still exit 2) while domains are OPEN local
# policy, so an unmapped domain falls back to the tier in silence — and because
# the token is interpolated into a variable name, its SHAPE ([a-z][a-z0-9-]*) is
# checked instead, exit 2 on anything else. UPDATING.md changed content too, for
# the 0.7.0 recipe delta. The wave's non-manifest half, recovered by Part 2:
# /to-tickets stamps an optional `Domain:` line when the medium of the work
# would change which model you would pick, /implement passes it as the second
# argument, the manual template's "Capability tiers" section explains the axis,
# and the claude-code adapter note works a domain-qualified spawn through. The
# consumer's own scripts/agents.config.sh (theirs, never overwritten) documents
# the AGENT_TIER_<TIER>_<DOMAIN> convention — existing consumers merge that
# comment hunk by hand, or skip it and lose nothing but the documentation.
# 0.8.0 — the transcripts became platform-independent. No file joined or left
# the layer; ONE shared file changed content: UPDATING.md, whose two worked
# examples are re-captured under LC_ALL=C. `sort` and `comm` order by the
# locale's collation, so the manifest lists inside those transcripts came out in
# one order on the machine that captured them (macOS, UTF-8: `UPDATING.md` after
# `scripts/...`) and another in the C locale (`UPDATING.md` before
# `constitution/...`). The dependence was latent from the day the transcripts
# existed and surfaced the day tests/docs-demo.sh gained a CI job, whose runner
# is the second platform the byte-for-byte comparison had ever seen. The
# capture pins themselves live in that suite — kit-only, not shared — so what
# a consumer takes here is the re-captured prose and nothing else: a re-read of
# the two worked examples, and no command of theirs changes.
# 0.9.0 — the self-hosting follow-ups. No file joined or left the layer; two
# shared files changed content. The substantive one, and only in its comments:
# scripts/docs-conformance/validators/claude-md-refs.mjs described "the kit's
# own unbootstrapped tree" as its example of a tree the shim and reachability
# rules stay silent on. The kit has self-hosted since ADR-0001, so that example
# named a state nothing is in any more — and a repo created from the template
# is not it either, since it carries the kit's own AGENTS.md and both shims
# until bootstrap strips them. The general statements were always right; only
# the example moved, to a tree whose manual has not been written yet. Zero
# behaviour change: the validator's 52 fixture tests pass unchanged, and a
# consumer taking 0.9.0 re-reads two comments and changes no command of theirs.
# The wave's non-manifest half is a kit-only test fix that a consumer never
# sees: tests/docs-demo.sh built its fake 0.3.0 consumer with a sed pinned to
# one literal version, so the rollback went silently dead the release after it
# was written and the fixture carried the CURRENT version instead. The marker
# line is now matched by SHAPE, and an assertion right after the fixture is
# built proves the rollback happened.
# The second content change is UPDATING.md, per this manifest's own precedent
# that a re-pin is a content change: its worked-example prose and transcripts
# moved 0.8.0 -> 0.9.0 with the release, nothing else. The recipe itself is
# untouched.
# 0.10.0 — the recipe stopped destroying consumer files. No file joined or left
# the layer; ONE shared file changed content, and materially: UPDATING.md. Both
# defects were found by the SECOND real consumer update (0.4.0 -> 0.9.0), and
# both are data loss, so a consumer on any release should take this one before
# running an update.
#   The first is a SHAPE, not a line. `kit show "$REF:$path" >"$mine"` was the
# recipe's idiom for taking the release's copy of a file, and the shell
# truncates `$mine` BEFORE kit is started — so a path that is absent at that ref
# leaves zero bytes and a `fatal:` on stderr. 9d is where it was caught: it
# named `scripts/docs-conformance/local-vocabulary.mjs`, which the kit ships
# only as a `.template`, asked whether the path existed at FROM_REF, and read
# that one `no` as "then it is new at TO_REF". Measured on a real consumer:
# 1807 bytes -> 0. Seven other sites carried the same shape. Step 0 now defines
# `kit_take`, which fetches to a temp file and writes only on success, and every
# take in the recipe goes through it. 9d additionally asks about BOTH refs and
# has a third verdict, STAMPED, for the half of its list the kit holds only as
# a `.template`.
#   The second is the shell. The recipe never declared one, and zsh — macOS's
# default — applies HISTORY MODIFIERS to `$var:x` inside double quotes, so
# `"$TO_REF:constitution/…"` was `$TO_REF` with `:c` applied plus the literal
# `onstitution/…`. Four lines carried a bare letter after the colon; git got a
# ref that resolves to nothing, one local article went 45 bytes -> 0, and the
# `cmp` above it answered YOURS having compared against empty input. Every ref
# expansion is now braced or followed by a `$`, "Before you start" names POSIX
# sh, and tests/docs-demo.sh greps the whole document for the shape on every
# run and drives 9b through a real zsh.
#   Three smaller recipe fixes ride along: 9d routes `.mjs` configs to a read of
# the diff rather than a key-set extractor that cannot parse them (and the
# extractor now refuses to answer rather than reporting a vacuous "nothing
# missing"); step 8 says that the kit's own self-hosted AGENTS.md, shims,
# README and docs are never a consumer's base, since those ARE paths a consumer
# has; and "Before you start" carries the standing "re-read this file after
# step 5" rule, which the step-5 NOTE cannot deliver to the consumer who most
# needs it because that consumer is on the old recipe.
#   What a consumer takes: a re-read of UPDATING.md, and one habit change — the
# takes in Part 2 are `kit_take` calls now, which needs step 0's helper in
# scope. Nothing else of theirs changes. That first change has no
# non-manifest half of its own.
#   The wave's second content change, landed before the v0.10.0 tag was cut so
# one release carries the incident AND its standing rules: shared-code-craft
# gains §11 (never point a redirect at a file you cannot lose) and §12 (declare
# the shell, then write for it) — the same two data-loss shapes, generalized
# from recipe fix to portable craft; UPDATING.md's step-0 "Pre-1.0" tag caveat
# retires (every release is tagged now, and the kit's self-host suite holds it
# to that). The non-manifest half this second change DOES have: /review-pr's
# Security Sentinel gains a shell-hazard audit citing the two rules by number.
# 0.11.0 — the skills adoption wave: VERSION gains the `skills:` section below,
# a NAME-LEVEL manifest of every skill this release ships. It exists because
# delta-based adoption failed in the field: /explain-diff shipped inside the
# v0.8.0 window, and a consumer whose update ranges never included that window
# lost the feature with no signal — shared layer byte-identical, gate green,
# and an entire PR-flow capability silently absent. Names, never bytes: a
# consumer is invited to adapt a skill's prose, so the manifest answers "do I
# HAVE it?", and only that. The update recipe diffs this list against the
# consumer's installed set — state, not delta, so skipped windows heal.
# UPDATING.md changed content with the bump (the worked-example transcripts
# and their surrounding prose re-pinned — a content change by this manifest's
# own precedent).
#   NON-MANIFEST HALF of this wave, enumerated (the discipline this release
# introduces — every release from here names what shipped outside the files:
# list): the skills manifest referee joined tests/ (kit-only); consumers on
# ≤0.10.0 adopting /explain-diff need the skill directory PLUS its two wiring
# points — the Deliver-phase appendix bullet in /implement and the
# explain-diff-appendix marker in the PR template. UPDATING.md moved with the
# manifest: step 9a now OPENS with the inventory fence (manifest vs installed,
# printed before any per-skill diff) and carries that retroactive note under
# "Arriving from 0.10.0 or older". The half-adopted STATE got its own detector:
# scripts/docs-conformance/validators/skill-web.mjs joined the layer — it scans
# installed skill bodies for references to skills that are not installed and
# WARNS, never fails (declining a skill is a legal recorded state; the gate's
# engine now separates advisories from violations, and the reduced POSIX form
# says it cannot run this scan). It reads claudeMdRefs.ignoreCommands, so one
# exemption list serves both validators — and that list gained two entries
# ("/tmp" and "/codebase-design", both command-shaped quoted non-references in
# the shipped skill prose). config.mjs is YOURS and is never overwritten, so
# an updating consumer must take those two entries by hand at step 9d, or the
# first gate run after the update warns on the kit's own skills.
# scripts/agents.lib.sh also changed content: the resolver's usage and
# unknown-tier messages no longer read a module global a sourced config could
# reassign — the four tier names are literals at every site, so the
# diagnostics can no longer disagree with the check. No behavior change to
# resolution, exit codes, or stdout. (This note grew with the wave's stacked
# tickets and was finished at the v0.11.0 tag.)
# 0.12.0 — skill interiors join the gate (#79, from K2's oldest caveat, #18).
# One file joined the layer: scripts/docs-conformance/validators/skill-paths.mjs.
# Every installed skill body — SKILL.md and the sidecar markdown beside it —
# now has its repo-path references resolved, as WARNINGS on the 0.11.0
# advisory channel. Warnings by discovery, not by softness: the spec ordered
# violations, and implementation found the legal reading — VERSION SKEW. The
# recipe is per-category and demands a green gate BEFORE an update starts, so
# a consumer mid-skew (skills referencing an article a later release
# delivers) is a sanctioned state the kit's own recipe demo models; a red
# gate would outlaw the starting point of every update.
# One rule covers both sides of bootstrap: a token
# resolves if the file exists OR its .template source does, so a skill naming
# a stamped article is right in a consumer and right in the kit. The grammar
# is claude-md-refs' own pathTokenRe/pathRefs over the same pathRoots — one
# definition of "what is a path reference", now three consumers.
#   NON-MANIFEST HALF, enumerated: your config.mjs (yours, never overwritten)
# gains a skillPaths section — exemptFiles for upstream-verbatim documents,
# and exemptTokens for paths that are legitimately absent until created (the
# shipped entries: the dogfood report directory, made by the skill's first
# run, and the example review workflow, installed by bootstrap from the
# templates tree). Take that section by hand at step 9d, or the gate warns on
# the kit's own shipped skills. The validator's fixture tests join the
# consumer-owned test directory alongside the others. One skill changed with
# the wave: /merge-train's step-5 release note no longer names the kit's own
# self-host suite by path — the first thing the new scan caught was the kit
# pointing every consumer at a file bootstrap deletes (take via 9a).
shared-layer: 0.12.0

# NOT shared layer, and deliberately so: scripts/guards.config.sh. It is the
# only file the guards read policy from, and its whole purpose is to be edited
# locally. Keeping mechanism shared and policy local is what lets the guard
# scripts stay byte-identical to the kit's copies.
#
# Same split, same reasoning, for capability tiers: scripts/agents.lib.sh is
# shared (one resolver, fixable once for everyone, and the four tier names are
# the shared word between the manual, the skills and the config) while
# scripts/agents.config.sh is NOT (it maps those tiers onto one provider's model
# identifiers — the fastest-rotting, least portable data in the repo). A kit
# that shared the mapping would be shipping a standing instruction with a timer
# on it.

